<h2>Equivalent Fractions on the Number Line — Original Source Families</h2>
<p>All trail runners refer to ONE equal-length trail. A fraction is an equal fraction of the same whole.</p>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Original pair</th><th>Distances</th><th>Same distance?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Elena and Han</th><td>3/6 vs 1/2</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Jada and Kiran</th><td>1/4 vs 2/8</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Lin and Mai</th><td>2/3 vs 5/6</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h3>Original eleven numbers</h3><p>1/2, 1/3, 1/4, 2/3, 2/6, 3/8, 3/4, 4/6, 4/8, 6/8, 7/8</p>
<p>These make the FOUR exact source equivalent pairs: 1/2=4/8; 1/3=2/6; 2/3=4/6; 3/4=6/8. Fractions 1/4, 3/8 and 7/8 have no second match IN THIS original eleven-item list. Students can construct other examples separately.</p>
<h3>Rolling for Equivalent Fractions — variable eight rounds</h3>
<p>Roll six number cubes. Dice showing 5 are wild and may take another chosen value. Use rolled numbers to build equivalent a/b=c/d; at most TWO rerolls may be used when needed. Complete EIGHT rounds with explanations. Teacher validates dice provenance, wildcard use, valid nonzero denominators, equal cross-products and the original student-created statements.</p>
<p>Example valid comparison: 1/2=2/4, conditional on those digits being rolled. Publisher art and restricted Cool-down are not copied. <a href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-5/lesson-12/lesson.html">Original 2021 teacher reference</a>.</p></section>